 inflation is easing toward target. They also say gradual reductions are possible over the next year if the labour market cools.\n\n**Market-implied path.** The cma_futures series (n4.d2) prices contracts at 100 minus the expected policy rate:\n\n| Quarter | Futures price | Implied rate |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2026Q4 | 95.5 | 4.50% |\n| 2027Q1 | 95.5 | 4.50% |\n| 2027Q2 | 95.75 | 4.25% |\n| 2027Q3 | 95.75 | 4.25% |\n| 2027Q4 | 96.0 | 4.00% |\n\nSo the market expects a hold through Q1 2027, a first cut by Q2, and a second by Q4, which is 50bp in total.\n\n**CMA guidance.** Governor Ashby said on 28 October that one 25bp cut in 2027 is the base case (doc cma-speech-ashby). She said markets \"may be getting ahead of themselves\" in pricing more. That implies a rate of about 4.25% rather than 4.00% at end-2027.\n\n**Gap and risk.** The market is pricing about one more cut than the CMA signals. If the CMA's view prevails, the futures price roughly 25bp too much easing, and rates stay higher than priced. Further cuts depend on the labour market cooling.\n\n**Limits of the data.** The futures curve ends at 2027Q4, so I have no market pricing for January 2028. I would treat 4.00–4.25% as the likely range, which assumes no further moves after Q4 2027. That is an assumption, not data.","ttft_ms":1885,"type":"result","duration_ms":23896,"uuid":"5b38318f-6fe8-4adc-8791-b97778340447","ttft_stream_ms":1028,"time_to_request_ms":62,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":5,"system_prompt":4,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":3,"input_attachments":11,"input_hooks":2,"conversation_append":0,"transcript_persist":3,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":5,"init_frame_send":1,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":3,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":0,"autocompact":1,"request_announcements":6,"query_setup":0,"model_gates":3,"tool_schema_build":3,"message_normalization":2,"client_creation":3,"request_build":5},"turn_start_resume_kind":"none","process_turn_index":0,"time_to_request_loop_lag_ms":11,"